course: dictionary
lesson: 14473
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Passed is a good example. It is the past tense of pass, so it is used for things that have already happened: «Sam passed the salt to his sister.» Here passed means gave something to someone.
explain: «The bus passed the school gate just as the bell rang.» Passed means went past, moved by something. Now read «Ruby passed her spelling test on Friday.» Passed means succeeded, so she did well. The same word does two different jobs. Ask yourself: what is happening in the sentence?
explain: «Weeks passed before the rain finally came.» Here passed means time went by. Some people also say «he passed» as a gentle way of saying someone has died. The words around it, and what the sentence is about, tell you which meaning is right.
fact: Passed is the past tense of the verb pass: pass, passes, passing, passed.
fact: «Passed» can mean went past, succeeded in a test, gave something to someone, or time went by.
fact: Do not mix up passed and past: «passed» is a verb about something that happened, while «past» is a different word, as in «walked past the shop».
fact: «Passed» is short for «passed away», a polite way of saying someone has died; it is used mostly in the USA.
fact: To choose the right meaning, read the clues around the word, then try your meaning in the sentence.
exercise: «The bus passed the school gate just as the bell rang.» What does passed mean here? | Gave away | *Went past | Failed | Stopped for ever
exercise: «Ruby passed her spelling test on Friday.» What does passed mean here? | *Succeeded in the test | Lost her way in the test | Went past the test room | Gave the test to a friend
exercise: «Weeks passed before the rain finally came.» What does passed mean here? | Weeks were broken into pieces | Weeks were handed out | *Time went by | People walked past the rain
test: «Please pass me the salt.» What does pass mean here? | *Give something to someone | Walk past without stopping | Succeed in a test | Time going by
test: «Tom passed by the park on his way home.» What does passed by mean here? | Gave the park away | Finished the park | *Went past | Failed a park test
exam: Which word means the same as passed in «Ella passed her maths test»? | *Succeeded | Lost | Forgot | Failed
